    labs: "Hb 11.5 g/dL (post-op drop). WBC 12.0 (post-op leukocytosis). CRP 45. Creatinine 0.9. LFTs normal.",
    imaging: "Post-operative X-ray: satisfactory alignment and hardware position.",
    diagnosis: "Acute Post-Operative Pain (Moderate-Severe) Following Tibial ORIF",
    ddx: ["Acute Surgical Pain", "Compartment Syndrome", "Wound Infection", "DVT"],
    goals: "Achieve adequate pain control (target pain score <4/10), facilitate early mobilisation, prevent chronic pain, minimise opioid use",
    pharm: "1. WHO Analgesic Ladder Step 2 (moderate pain not controlled by Step 1): Add Tramadol 50-100mg PO/IM q6h PRN (max 400mg/day). 2. Continue Paracetamol 1g QID (scheduled, not PRN). 3. Continue Diclofenac 75mg IM/PO BID (max 5-7 days, add PPI cover). 4. If pain still uncontrolled, escalate to WHO Step 3: Morphine 5-10mg PO/SC q4h PRN. 5. Neuropathic component: add Gabapentin 300mg at bedtime (or Pregabalin 75mg BID) if nerve injury suspected. 6. Laxative (Lactulose 15mL BID or Senna) if on opioids. 7. Antiemetic (Ondansetron 4mg TID PRN) for opioid nausea.",
    nonPharm: "Elevation of left leg. Ice packs to surgical site 20 mins on/off. Early physiotherapy for mobilisation. Relaxation techniques. Adequate sleep hygiene.",
    carePlan: "Review analgesic regimen: switch from PRN to scheduled dosing for better pain control. Add Tramadol. Start PPI. Monitor pain scores q4h. If pain controlled, transition to oral analgesics and prepare for discharge with physiotherapy.",
    dtps: "1. Inadequate post-operative pain control requiring escalation of analgesia. 2. Opioid use requires monitoring for side effects (constipation, nausea, respiratory depression).",
    monitoring: "Pain score (NRS 0-10) at rest and with movement q4h. Sedation score if on opioids. Bowel function chart. Respiratory rate if on morphine. Watch for signs of compartment syndrome (pain out of proportion, paraesthesia, pallor, pulselessness, poikilothermia).",
    counselling: "Pain control is important for healing and recovery — do not try to \"tough it out.\" Take pain medications on a schedule (not PRN) for the first few days. As pain improves, we will reduce and stop the stronger medications. Physiotherapy and movement are also important for pain relief. Report any new severe pain, numbness, or tingling.",
    followUp: "Orthopaedic clinic in 2 weeks for wound check and X-ray. Continue physiotherapy. Gradually reduce analgesia over 1-2 weeks as pain improves.",
    pearls: "The WHO Analgesic Ladder is a stepwise approach: Step 1 (non-opioid ± adjuvant) → Step 2 (weak opioid + non-opioid ± adjuvant) → Step 3 (strong opioid + non-opioid ± adjuvant). Paracetamol and NSAIDs have a ceiling effect — increasing the dose beyond maximum does not increase analgesia. Scheduled dosing is superior to PRN for acute pain. Multimodal analgesia (using drugs with different mechanisms) improves pain control and reduces opioid requirements.",
    references: ["WHO Guidelines: Pain Management", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Acute Pain", "BJA: Multimodal Analgesia"],
