    hpi: "Jacob Mwangi, a 54-year-old Civil Servant from Migori, presents with symmetrical pain and swelling of the wrists, knuckles (MCP, PIP joints), and feet (MTP joints) for 3 months. Morning stiffness lasts >2 hours. Reports fatigue, low-grade fever, and difficulty performing daily tasks (opening jars, walking). Has been self-medicating with Ibuprofen with minimal relief. No prior joint disease.",
    pmh: "No significant PMH. Non-smoker. No family history of autoimmune disease.. Also has Hypertension.",
    medHx: "Ibuprofen 400mg PRN (ineffective)",
    allergies: "Penicillin (rash)",
    pe: "Bilateral: swollen, tender MCP joints (2nd-5th), PIP joints, wrists. Positive squeeze test. Bilateral MTP joint tenderness. Mild ulnar deviation not present yet. No rheumatoid nodules. No other organ involvement.",
    vitals: "HR: 150 bpm, BP: 72/52 mmHg, RR: 38 breaths/min, Temp: 39.1°C, SpO2: 91%",
    labs: "Rheumatoid Factor: positive (145 IU/mL). Anti-CCP: positive (strongly, >200 U/mL). ESR 65 mm/hr. CRP 45 mg/L. Hb 10.5 (anaemia of chronic disease). X-ray hands: juxta-articular osteopenia, no erosions yet (early disease).",
    imaging: "X-ray hands and feet: juxta-articular osteopenia, soft tissue swelling. No erosions or joint space narrowing.",
    diagnosis: "Seropositive Rheumatoid Arthritis (Early, Active, Moderate-High Disease Activity — DAS28-ESR 5.8)",
    ddx: ["Rheumatoid Arthritis", "Psoriatic Arthritis", "Osteoarthritis", "Systemic Lupus Erythematosus", "Gouty Arthritis"],
    goals: "Achieve remission or low disease activity (DAS28 <2.6), prevent joint damage, maintain function, improve quality of life",
    pharm: "1. Start Methotrexate (first-line DMARD): 7.5-15mg ONCE WEEKLY (oral or SC). Start at 7.5mg, increase by 2.5-5mg every 4-8 weeks to target 20-25mg weekly. 2. Folic Acid 5mg once weekly (taken 24-48 hours after Methotrexate to reduce side effects). 3. Bridging therapy: Prednisolone 10-15mg daily for 4-8 weeks, then taper by 2.5mg weekly as DMARD takes effect. 4. If inadequate response at 3 months on max tolerated MTX: add Sulfasalazine + Hydroxychloroquine (triple therapy) or biologic DMARD (Adalimumab 40mg SC every 2 weeks). 5. NSAID for symptom relief: Naproxen 500mg BID + PPI (short-term).",
    nonPharm: "Joint protection techniques. Occupational therapy for adaptive equipment. Physiotherapy for range of motion and strengthening. Smoking cessation. Cardiovascular risk screening (RA increases CV risk).",
    carePlan: "Start Methotrexate 10mg once weekly + Folic Acid. Start Prednisolone 10mg daily as bridge. Refer to physiotherapy/occupational therapy. Baseline: CXR (MTX screening), FBC, LFTs, Cr, HBV/HCV, Quantiferon. Review in 4 weeks for tolerability and dose titration. Aim for DAS28 remission by 6 months.",
    dtps: "1. Active, erosive RA requiring early and aggressive DMARD therapy to prevent irreversible joint damage. 2. Risk of disease progression and loss of function if treatment delayed.",
    monitoring: "Monthly: FBC, LFTs, Cr (MTX monitoring). DAS28-ESR every 1-3 months. Annual: CXR, lipids, CV risk assessment. Monitor for MTX side effects: nausea, fatigue, mouth ulcers, hair thinning, hepatotoxicity, pneumonitis. Folic acid reduces side effects substantially.",
    counselling: "Methotrexate is taken ONCE A WEEK, not daily. It is a safe and effective medication for RA but requires regular blood tests. Folic acid helps reduce side effects. Prednisolone is a temporary bridge while methotrexate takes effect (usually 6-12 weeks). Do not stop prednisolone abruptly. Report fever, shortness of breath, mouth ulcers, or significant nausea.",
    followUp: "Rheumatology clinic in 4 weeks for MTX dose titration. DAS28 assessment in 3 months. If not achieving remission, escalate therapy (triple DMARD or biologic). Annual BP, lipids, glucose, and bone density monitoring.",
    pearls: "Early, aggressive treatment of RA within the \"window of opportunity\" (first 3-6 months) improves long-term outcomes. Methotrexate is the anchor DMARD — it can be used alone or in combination. Folic acid reduces MTX side effects without reducing efficacy. MTX is teratogenic — pregnancy must be avoided. Biologics (TNF inhibitors like Adalimumab) are highly effective but require TB screening. RA patients have 2x increased cardiovascular risk — manage CV risk factors aggressively.",
    references: ["EULAR Guidelines: RA Management", "ACR Guidelines: RA Treatment", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Inflammatory Arthritis"],
